type: framework
namespace: FCA
version: "2026-08"
anchor_version: ">=6.0.0"
maintainer: "Anchor Core"
opt_in: true
source: >
  FCA "AI Update" (2024); Senior Managers and Certification Regime
  (SM&CR); FCA Handbook SYSC (Systems and Controls) sourcebook,
  particularly SYSC 4.1.1R and SYSC 3; FCA Consumer Duty (PS22/9,
  FG22/5); Money Laundering Regulations 2017.
source_url: "https://www.fca.org.uk/firms/innovation/ai-approach"
source_date: "2016-2026"
credit: >
  The FCA has stated explicitly and repeatedly that it will not
  introduce AI-specific rules. Its 2024 "AI Update" sets out how
  EXISTING regimes — SYSC governance rules, SM&CR individual
  accountability, and Consumer Duty — already apply to AI systems.
  This framework file maps Anchor enforcement to those existing,
  binding regimes as applied to AI, rather than to any AI-specific
  rulebook, because none currently exists.
verification_note: >
  MAJOR CORRECTIONS from prior version, found by checking each citation
  against primary/authoritative sources rather than accepting the
  prior file's descriptions:
    (1) FCA-001 and FCA-002 previously cited "FCA AI Governance Guidance
        2024" and linked to FS23/6. FS23/6 (Oct 26, 2023) is a Feedback
        Statement summarising industry responses to a 2022 discussion
        paper — multiple independent legal sources confirm it "does not
        include policy proposals" and imposes no requirements at all.
        It cannot be cited as requiring anything. Corrected to cite the
        actual applicable source: SYSC governance rules and SM&CR,
        as clarified by the FCA's real 2024 "AI Update" publication.
    (2) FCA-003 previously cited "FCA Guidance September 2026" for CCO/
        CRO personal liability. No such document exists as of this
        version's date. What IS real: SM&CR has imposed personal
        accountability on named senior managers since 2016; FCA
        Executive Director David Geale told the Treasury Committee in
        January 2026 that individuals are "on the hook" for AI harm
        under EXISTING SM&CR rules; the "Mills Review" was expected to
        report to the FCA Board in summer 2026 (not yet confirmed
        published as of this version); the Treasury Committee has
        called for the FCA to publish comprehensive SM&CR/AI guidance
        by end of 2026. Corrected below to cite this real basis instead
        of a fabricated document.
    (3) FCA-004 previously cited "FCA AML Guidance 2024" as requiring
        AI-generated AML outputs to be "cryptographically linkable" to
        model outputs. No such specific technical requirement could be
        found in any FCA publication. The FCA's actual stated AML/AI
        position is that it "regulate[s] the outcome, rather than the
        technology used to obtain it" (explainability and accuracy
        expected, specific implementation not mandated). Corrected to
        frame the cryptographic binding as Anchor's own reasonable
        technical implementation of the general explainability/audit
        expectation under MLR 2017 and existing SYSC record-keeping
        rules — not as a quotation of a specific FCA technical mandate.
    (4) FCA-005 (Consumer Duty) verified accurate as previously written
        — PS22/9, the four outcomes, and the July 31 2023 effective
        date all confirmed against the primary FCA policy statement.
        Retained with primitives added.
layer_2_status: >
  Rules marked obligation_type: provenance or audit depend on
  AnchorRuntime (Layer 2) and the Decision Audit Chain (DAC).
  Layer 2 is currently in development.
seal: "sha256:PENDING"

rules:

  - id: "FCA-001"
    name: "Human Oversight Records — AI-Assisted Decisions"
    original_id: "SYSC 4.1.1R (governance arrangements) and SM&CR Conduct Rule 2, as applied to AI per the FCA's 2024 AI Update (corrected — previously miscited to FS23/6, which imposes no requirements)"
    maps_to: "ETH-003"
    obligation_type: audit
    primitives:
      action: demonstrate
      object: human_oversight_of_ai_decision
      context: existing_governance_rules_applied_to_ai
      authority: fca_via_sysc_and_smcr
      flow: oversight_action_to_documented_record
    anchor_mechanism: >
      DAC query API /audit exposes full oversight record. ETH-003
      human oversight removal violation fires on autonomous decision
      code without a human review checkpoint. AuditEntry records a
      compliant boolean per decision. Layer 2 in development.
    severity: "error"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      The FCA has not issued AI-specific rules, but has stated clearly
      that existing governance obligations — SYSC 4.1.1R (adequate
      governance arrangements) and SM&CR's Conduct Rule 2 (a named
      senior manager must take reasonable steps to control the business
      they're responsible for) — apply fully to AI systems. In practice
      this means firms should be able to demonstrate human oversight of
      AI-assisted decisions with actual records, not merely a policy
      statement claiming oversight occurs. Severity set to "error"
      rather than the prior version's "blocker" to reflect that this is
      Anchor's reasonable interpretation of existing rules applied to
      AI, not a quoted AI-specific mandate.

  - id: "FCA-002"
    name: "Model Version Traceability Per Decision"
    original_id: "SYSC 3 (systems and controls) as applied to AI per the FCA's 2024 AI Update (corrected — previously miscited to FS23/6)"
    maps_to: "SUP-003"
    obligation_type: provenance
    primitives:
      action: trace
      object: model_version_per_decision
      context: existing_systems_and_controls_rules
      authority: fca_via_sysc
      flow: model_version_change_to_continuous_traceability
    anchor_mechanism: >
      model_version in every AuditEntry records exact model version
      per decision. SUP-003 versioning drift violation fires on
      undeclared model version changes. Layer 2 in development.
    severity: "warning"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      Existing SYSC systems-and-controls obligations imply that firms
      should be able to trace which version of which model produced
      which decision, continuously — not merely at initial deployment.
      This is a reasonable, defensible implementation of existing
      record-keeping obligations as applied to AI, not a quotation of
      an AI-specific requirement — no such specific requirement was
      found in any FCA publication. Severity lowered from prior
      version's "error" to reflect this.

  - id: "FCA-003"
    name: "Senior Manager Personal Accountability for AI Systems (SM&CR)"
    original_id: "SM&CR Conduct Rule 2 (existing since 2016); FCA Executive Director David Geale, oral evidence to House of Commons Treasury Committee, January 2026 (corrected — previously cited a non-existent 'FCA Guidance September 2026')"
    maps_to: "ETH-003"
    obligation_type: disclosure
    primitives:
      action: demonstrate
      object: senior_manager_ai_oversight
      context: existing_smcr_conduct_rule_2
      authority: named_senior_management_function_holder
      flow: ai_system_behavior_to_personal_accountability_evidence
    anchor_mechanism: >
      Signed DAC audit chain is the evidence a named Senior Manager
      would present of active oversight. anchor audit --report
      generates the compliance record demonstrating governance during
      the relevant period. A Senior Manager who can produce sealed,
      timestamped governance records has documented evidence
      supporting their Duty of Responsibility under SM&CR.
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "error"
    description: >
      SM&CR has imposed personal accountability on named Senior
      Management Function holders since 2016 — a regulatory breach in
      their area shifts the burden to them to show they took reasonable
      steps to prevent, identify, or stop it. This is NOT new: what's
      new is the FCA's explicit confirmation that it applies to AI
      without dilution. FCA Executive Director David Geale told the
      Treasury Committee in January 2026 that individuals are "on the
      hook" for AI-caused consumer harm under existing rules — not
      future ones. The "Mills Review" was expected to report to the FCA
      Board in summer 2026 with more detailed guidance; the Treasury
      Committee separately called for the FCA to publish comprehensive
      SM&CR/AI guidance by end of 2026. Watch for either landing and
      update this rule's citation accordingly — but the underlying
      personal-liability exposure is real and current today, under
      SM&CR as it already stands, not contingent on either of those.

  - id: "FCA-004"
    name: "AML AI Output Verifiability"
    original_id: "Money Laundering Regulations 2017; general FCA AML/AI position (corrected — previously cited a nonexistent 'FCA AML Guidance 2024' technical mandate)"
    maps_to: "SEC-006"
    obligation_type: provenance
    primitives:
      action: verify
      object: ai_generated_aml_flag
      context: outcome_focused_not_technology_prescribed
      authority: fca_via_mlr_2017
      flow: model_output_to_traceable_verification
    anchor_mechanism: >
      AML-002 violation fires on AML output without output_hash
      verification. output_hash in AuditEntry provides SHA-256 binding
      of every AML flagging result to the model output that produced
      it. This is Anchor's own technical implementation choice for
      satisfying the general verifiability expectation below — not a
      quotation of an FCA-mandated cryptographic standard, since no
      such specific standard was found in FCA publications. Layer 2 in
      development.
    severity: "warning"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      The FCA's actual stated position on AI in AML is outcome-focused:
      it "regulate[s] the outcome, rather than the technology used to
      obtain it," while expecting AI-driven AML outcomes to remain
      "accurate, risk-based, and explainable" under MLR 2017's existing
      transaction-monitoring and record-keeping obligations. Anchor's
      cryptographic output-hash binding is a defensible, verifiable way
      to satisfy that expectation — but it should be described as
      Anchor's implementation choice, not as a specific technique the
      FCA has mandated. Severity lowered from prior version's "error"
      to reflect the outcome-focused (not technology-prescriptive)
      nature of the actual regulatory expectation.

  - id: "FCA-005"
    name: "Consumer Duty — Good Outcomes for Retail Customers"
    original_id: "FCA Consumer Duty, Policy Statement PS22/9 and Finalised Guidance FG22/5 (verified accurate)"
    maps_to: "ETH-004"
    obligation_type: detection
    primitives:
      action: deliver
      object: good_outcome_for_retail_customer
      context: four_outcome_areas_products_price_understanding_support
      authority: fca_principle_12
      flow: ai_output_to_consumer_outcome_evidence
    anchor_mechanism: >
      ETH-004 toxic output detection fires on harmful customer
      communications. ETH-002 explainability absence fires on opaque
      customer-facing AI decisions. SHR-003 reputational and conduct
      risk covers Consumer Duty conduct obligations. Layer 1 active
      now.
    severity: "error"
    min_severity: "error"
    description: >
      Verified against the primary FCA policy statement: Principle 12
      requires firms to "act to deliver good outcomes for retail
      customers," operationalised through three cross-cutting rules
      (act in good faith, avoid causing foreseeable harm, enable
      customers to pursue their financial objectives) and four outcome
      areas (products and services, price and value, consumer
      understanding, consumer support). In effect since July 31, 2023
      for new/existing products, extended to closed "legacy" books
      July 31, 2024. For AI systems interacting with retail customers,
      this means outputs must be accurate, fair, and comprehensible —
      not optimized for firm metrics at customer expense. This rule's
      content and citation were accurate in the prior version; retained
      with primitives added.
